# Ni - Ugur interatomic potential, CANDIDATE, force-matched, switched
#
# pair_style ugur
# pair_coeff * * Ni_force_taper.ugur Ni
#
# Truncation: switched from 0.85 of each cutoff to the cutoff, quintic, C2 - energy is conserved in MD: measured drift 0.4 meV/atom/ns for copper, 876x less than its hard twin.  USE for molecular dynamics, which no hard set can do.  It pays for that on the dispersion: the switched sets average 12.6 % against the hard sets' 9.7 %, because switching the pair term off over the outer 15 % moves the force constants at the largest separations, which is the short-wavelength end, and nothing in the fit sees it
# MD: not screened.
#
# Lattice stability: screened on the 8^3 and 9^3 meshes AND along the
# symmetry path; no imaginary modes.
#
# THREE-BODY CEILING: this set is OVER it.
# E3/E2 = 0.3001 against the bound of 0.30, exceeding it by 0.0005.
# standalone/fit.py applies that bound with no tolerance, so this solution
# would not have been accepted by the fit that produced the shipped sets.
#
# Nudge test: not run.
#
# Fitted to: DFT FORCES on displaced supercells of Ni, from the
# database cited here, with the cohesive energy, lattice constant and
# elastic constants kept as anchors.
# Source: Fellman, Byggmastar, Granberg, Nordlund, Djurabekova, Phys. Rev. Materials 9, 053807 (2025).
#
# Format is LAMMPS's Tersoff convention - one line per ordered triple
# (centre, leg, leg) - so that this file and a multi-element one are read the
# same way.  For a single element that is one line.
#
# el1 el2 el3  m D alpha r0 gamma C alpha3 rcut2 rcut3 taper lam2 lam4
Ni Ni Ni 2.590629196566538 0.5824947300818083 0.92504207664227445 2.7728198691422814 0.69268447816319156 -0.027981780709787449 0.43143554770769665 5.2033292183691886 5.1288501542427429 0.84999999999999998 0 0
